2186045417] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1033 232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6197c039-5830-56a2-b171-eac0f7516ad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1031 "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1033 1868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1034 0.5951061045948267 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050996 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7821b684-8118-5d42-9459-ebb20010e0ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133701 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133701 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ for every matrix $A?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A)$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(A^T)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133701 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47945f1d-ab72-5fe8-bc2c-43a69480b323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133701 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ is the row space of $A,$ and consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $A.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $A,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $A.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $A,$ we multiply each row of $A$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133703 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9099" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133703 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = 3,$ then find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133703 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb597435-f43e-54de-a219-1af646efcb82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133703 #edb/ref 1074 "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=8,$ $n=6,$ and $n-r=3.$ \n\nSubstituting $n=6$ into $n-r=3,$ we can find the rank of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n6 - r = 3 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nr = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T})) & =r=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133704 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133704 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $m \\times n$ matrix. What is the image of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ under the action of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133704 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd1f48b4-8bfc-536d-8145-0effcf44a788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133704 #edb/ref 1074 "Pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A{:}$\n\n![](images/dc/dc853850b03dd2cf8fcf0168392e3490423725120b6acb55f88028a702d30fa5.svg)\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under the action of $A$ must be $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(A)$ is mapped to the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ must be $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8b08bdf79d53f312cabd665bcd4669795bd8851dfb33d5e5db6178bd80b24398.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133885 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133887 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1a36c7f7-4287-562b-94ea-047b38ede70f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133888 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1045 "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134041 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "6fb1a6a6-6d39-5ea2-af30-b6e13fa3c1ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134076 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $7\\times 5$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))=4,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T}))=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "485e3060-5691-5e86-bcb1-ec2efd550199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134076 #edb/ref 1074 "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=7,$ $n=5,$ and $r=4.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T})) & =m-r=7-4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134834 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134834 #edb/ref 1061 33903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134834 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f59f0795-5e5a-517e-8142-7251b3810d58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134834 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134835 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134835 #edb/ref 1061 33904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134835 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a063cde8-8349-5bde-8239-ea3b6c3146eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134835 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134837 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134837 #edb/ref 1061 33918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134837 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f05c128d-c2b3-55a6-b5af-aa01107d4052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134837 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134838 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134838 #edb/ref 1061 33937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134838 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f70134b1-0b93-5597-a6b6-16fdc7fb1bad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134838 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134839 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134839 #edb/ref 1061 33950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134839 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8b74e81-7593-5f0a-9136-22383330a688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134839 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134840 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134840 #edb/ref 1061 33951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134840 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "957cde37-32d4-515e-ad96-75c96f7bfa57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134840 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134841 #edb/ref 1061 33952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134841 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8eef352e-273a-5614-b97d-8c357807e9a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134841 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135110 #edb/ref 1064 "When considering an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ as a linear transformation\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^n \\xrightarrow{\\quad A \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^m,\n$$\n\nthere are **four fundamental subspaces** that naturally occur. The first two are as follows:\n\n- The **column space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ This is the set of all linear combinations of the columns of $A.$\n\n- The **null space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is the set of all vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that, when pre-multiplied by $A,$ result in the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nNow, notice that the transpose $A^T$ is an $n \\times m$ matrix and can be considered as the transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^m \\xrightarrow{\\quad A^T \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nSo, we have similar subspaces for the transpose $A^T{:}$\n\n- The column space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ We call this the **row space** of $A$ since the span of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to the span of the rows of $A.$\n\n- The null space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ We call this the **left null space** of $A$ since it is equivalent to the set of all vectors $\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^m$ such that $\\mathbf y^T A = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's discuss how these spaces relate to each other:\n\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that are orthogonal complements of each other:\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A^T) = \\text{Null}(A)^\\perp\n$$\n\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ that are orthogonal complements of each other:\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A) = \\text{Null}(A^T)^\\perp\n$$\n\nThe four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/ced4191955b6886491bf612c62344d2faf6e7fe8d49884446de0eb86851d22e8.png)\n\nTo see why $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ note the following:\n\n- When calculating the product $A^T \\mathbf x,$ we multiply each row of $A^T$ by $\\mathbf x.$\n\n- Suppose that $\\mathbf x\\in \\text{Null}(A^T).$ Then, by definition, $A^T \\mathbf x = \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Thus, $\\mathbf x$ must be orthogonal to each row of $A^T.$ Equivalently, this means that $\\mathbf x$ is orthogonal to each *column* of $A.$\n\n- Hence, $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nWe can deduce that $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ by similar reasoning." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135110 #edb/ref 1065 5443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135110 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2b6f45b1-7cfa-5c62-9928-caeff36d1cc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135110 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135111 #edb/ref 1064 "Given an $m \\times n$ matrix $A,$ what is the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under $A?$\n\nNote that pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3cf2fb6734efe387ad41c59743385154d7a172d2cc10ee0fe69ac6a7f2b342a.svg)\n\nTherefore, by definition, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$\n\n![](images/db/dbefb436195af5ffa8841f4119054426db50ac2987138a5912991cf42321e0dc.png)\n\nSimilarly, pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$ by $A^T$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A^T{:}$\n![](images/60/60574ae7f9fc09522e9d725c787dce63228c67e19cbef002b14e29c1f27e4b20.svg)\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^m$ under the action of $A^T$ must be $\\text{Col}(A^T).$ This is also the row space of $A,$ since a linear combination of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to a linear combination of the rows of $A.$\n\n![](images/c8/c82ab88289fc2d35ce354933937d908c54a9839bfa0fd126187a9723c58eeb27.png)\n\nWe can use similar reasoning to find the image of the four fundamental subspaces under the appropriate matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135111 #edb/ref 1065 5454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135111 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "915420e9-f241-5e99-8274-32ffdfb79b9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135111 #edb/ref 1067 "Images Under the Action of a Matrix and Its Transpose" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135112 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with rank $r.$ The dimensions of the four fundamental subspaces of $A$ are as follows:\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A)) = r.$ Since the column space is the span of the columns of $A,$ its dimension is the number of linearly independent columns which, by Gaussian elimination, is $\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T)) = r.$ It can be shown that the dimension of the row space of $A$ also equals $r.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(A^T)=\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = n-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)}_{n} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T))}_{r} = n-r.\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = m-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^m)}_{m} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))}_{r} = m-r.\n$$\n\nLet's add these dimensions to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c550b87048bba6a7c362f00f09faca74ca9b056a93698436d0994c120971356.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135112 #edb/ref 1065 5455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135112 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "40af35b2-abb6-59b8-999a-9976edbb9ce8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135112 #edb/ref 1067 "Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135113 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's review what we have learned in this lesson.\n\nIf we consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ of rank $r$ as a transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ then there are four fundamental subspaces of $A{:}$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ with dimensions $r$ and $m-r,$ respectively.\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ and $\\text{Null}(A)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with dimensions $r$ and $n-r,$ respectively.\n\nFurthermore,\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ and\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$ and $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ and\n\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A)$ under the action of $A^T$ is $\\text{Col}(A^T).$\n\nThese facts about the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/37/37f633d0bca1a599f7d1658c051f802da5d607d420beeb4bddbef5cd98c366ef.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135113 #edb/ref 1065 5456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135113 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "190e1777-4f27-5c94-88bd-21c9992eb024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135113 #edb/ref 1067 "The Big Picture of the Four Fundamental Subspaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362710 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470056 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362710 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470057 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362710 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470058 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362710 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362710 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362710 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ for every matrix $A?$\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(A)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Null}(A)$\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362710 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b12af04-ad27-575e-b0d1-aaf05c9b69e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362710 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ is the left null space of $A.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${A}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$, that is, each column of $A$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362717 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470059 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362717 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470060 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362717 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470061 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362717 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362717 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362717 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ for every matrix $B?$\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(B)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(B)$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362717 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90aa20cc-7bd4-5b9c-96fe-e3f55743e36c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362717 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ is the row space of $B.$ This consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $B.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $B,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $B.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $B,$ we actually multiply each row of $B$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Null}(B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362730 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362730 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362730 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $4\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))=1,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T}))=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362730 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa262e05-5582-5f00-bdc8-007681456bff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362730 #edb/ref 1074 "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=4,$ $n=3,$ and $n-r=1.$ \n\nSubstituting $n=3$ into $n-r=1,$ we can find the rank of $A:$\n\n$$\n(3)-r=1\\,⟹\\,r=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T})) & =r=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362731 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362731 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362731 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362731 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362731 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362731 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362731 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362731 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186362733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362731 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ff003ae2-5c05-524e-9932-2c06214cec6